From: SM-G955N Ubuntu port contributors
Subject: [PATCH] proc: accept invisible as the existing hidepid=2 policy

Modern systemd tries hidepid=invisible. The pinned kernel accepts numeric
hidepid=0/1/2 but rejects the named spelling. The kernel proc documentation
specifies invisible as the name of hidepid=2, not hidepid=1 (noaccess):
https://docs.kernel.org/filesystems/proc.html#mount-options

Add only this exact spelling and select the existing value 2. Preserve
numeric parsing/range validation, gid parsing and all access checks.
Unknown/unsupported policies still fail. No new procfs isolation model is
backported: this kernel still stores the policy in the PID namespace,
so this alias retains the same scope and effects as numeric hidepid=2.

Local compatibility correction; not an upstream patch backport.

diff --git a/fs/proc/root.c b/fs/proc/root.c
--- a/fs/proc/root.c
+++ b/fs/proc/root.c
@@ -39,11 +39,12 @@
 }
 
 enum {
-	Opt_gid, Opt_hidepid, Opt_err,
+	Opt_gid, Opt_hidepid, Opt_hidepid_invisible, Opt_err,
 };
 
 static const match_table_t tokens = {
 	{Opt_hidepid, "hidepid=%u"},
+	{Opt_hidepid_invisible, "hidepid=invisible"},
 	{Opt_gid, "gid=%u"},
 	{Opt_err, NULL},
 };
@@ -69,6 +70,10 @@
 			if (match_int(&args[0], &option))
 				return 0;
 			pid->pid_gid = make_kgid(current_user_ns(), option);
+			break;
+		case Opt_hidepid_invisible:
+			/* Same access policy as the existing numeric hidepid=2. */
+			pid->hide_pid = 2;
 			break;
 		case Opt_hidepid:
 			if (match_int(&args[0], &option))
